PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md §4.1: "صمم evidence bundle read-only: manifest،
schema/app version، recorder capabilities، events/incidents/rules ذات الصلة،
checksums، optional signature، وبدون أسرار أو أسماء DNS ما لم يوافق
المستخدم. نفذ import في DB مؤقت ثم atomic promote؛ لا يلمس السجل الأصلي.
أضف export redacted."

Added internal/bundle/ (bundle.go + bundle_test.go, 10 tests, all passing on
Windows and Linux with -race). Format: tar.gz containing manifest.json,
events.json, incidents.json, SHA256SUMS, and an optional SHA256SUMS.sig -
deliberately the same checksum-file shape internal/update already parses
(ParseChecksums) and the release process already produces, so the project has
one checksum format, not two.

What's real and tested:
- Export reads a window of events+incidents from any store.Store, redacts
  DNS query names by default (the one field docs/schema.md already treats as
  sensitive), and writes the archive with a manifest recording exactly what
  it contains - including a Truncated flag if the window held more than the
  read limit, so a bundle never silently looks more complete than it is.
- Import verifies checksums before parsing anything, verifies a signature if
  (and only if) a public key is configured - same mandatory-once-configured
  policy as internal/update, reusing its VerifySignature directly rather than
  a second implementation - then validates every event individually
  (invalid ones are dropped and counted, never fatal to the whole import),
  and only then promotes a fully-built temporary SQLite file into place with
  a single rename. It never opens or touches any other store on the machine.
- Zip-slip: extraction only ever writes into a fixed, five-name allow-list
  keyed by filepath.Base(entry name), so a crafted archive entry with "../"
  or an absolute path is inert by construction, not by a check that could be
  forgotten later. Tested by actually adding such an entry to a real bundle
  and confirming import still succeeds correctly, ignoring it.
- Signing is deliberately NOT built into Export: the same reasoning as
  internal/update's signing key never touching the running process (Makefile
  "sign" target) applies here - a bundle is signed the same external way a
  release is, and Import's verification is what matters, tested against both
  a correctly and an incorrectly signed bundle.

Full repo test suite and go vet, Windows and Linux (the latter with -race):
clean.
